    "updated_at": "2021-05-01T01:36:19Z",
    "body": "I'm not really sure what the semantics of `scan_for_user_config` is in that example. Technically it's all \"user config\" it's just that one is a global config file and the other is an environment variables.\r\n\r\nI just had a quick look at that config value for the python module. Following the naming of the python config option maybe this makes more sense.\r\n\r\n```toml\r\n[gcloud]\r\nscan_for_env_var = true # disabling this would ignore CLOUDSDK_ACTIVE_CONFIG_NAME and only use the config file\r\nscan_for_config_file = true # disabling this would ignore the config file and only use the CLOUDSDK_ACTIVE_CONFIG_NAME\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe reason I mentioned the other option of supporting a list of enum flag is that I think something like a `scan_for = []` option would have the ability to be much more portable across different modules instead of slowly accumulating a growing list of module-specific boolean flags.\r\n\r\nEG:\r\n\r\n```toml\r\n[gcloud]\r\nscan_for = [\"env_var\", \"global_config_file\"] # default scan for both sources of config\r\n# OR\r\nscan_for = [\"env_var\"] # scan for just environment variables for config\r\n# OR\r\nscan_for = [\"global_config_file\"] # scan for just the global config_file for config\r\n```\r\n\r\nEG intead of  `scan_for_pyfiles = true` you could have:\r\n\r\n```toml\r\n[python]\r\nscan_for = [\"project_files\", \"py_files\"] # display python module when python project files or any .py files are present\r\n# OR\r\nscan_for = [\"py_files\"]  # only use the precense of .py files to determine if the python module should be enabled\r\n# OR\r\nscan_for = [\"project_files\"]  # only use the precense of typical python project files to determine if the python module should be enabled (eg `requirements.txt` `.python-version` etc)\r\n```",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
